//initialize handshake
// client -> server
{ 
    "jsonrpc": "2.0",
    "id": 0,
    "method": "initialize",
    "params": {
        "protocolVersion": "2025-06-18",
        "capabilities": {
            "sampling": {}
        },
        "clientinfo": {
            "name": "my-agent-host",
            "version": "1.1.0"
        }
    }
}
// Server -> client
{
    "jsonrpc": "2.0",
    "id": 0,
    "result": {
        "protocolVersion": "2025-06-18",
        "capabilities": {
            "tools": {
                "listChanged": true
            },
            "resources": {
                 // Information the agent can read
                "subscribe": true,
                "listChanged": true
            }
        },
        "serverInfo": {
            "name": "saleforce-mcp-server",
            "version": "0.4.1"
        }
    }
}


// What can the client pass vs what the server cna pass

/**

Client can send tool discovery: find available tools, Tool invocation: evecute the tool
with arguments. Resource discovery: Discover available resources. 
Resources retrieval: resources/read: read a source by uri
Prompt discovery: promots/list
Prompt retrieval: get a prompt with arguments
Initialisation: Negotiate capabilities with protocol version.
So , client sends the initialize, tool discovery, find tool, tool invoke with arguments, 
prompt discovery and retrieval 
Client has 3 things only - sampling, roots (permitted workspace roots or directories) , elicitation
(asl the user for missing information or confirmation)

Server can send: 
a. Tool definition, b.tool results,c. resoources (1.uri, 2.metadata , 3.resource contents, 4.prompts, 
5.notification, additonal input requests)

Server advertises a tool
{
    "name" :"get_user_permission_sets",
    "description": "retrieve an user's permission sets",
    "input schema" : {
        "type" : "objecty",
        "propertoies" : {
            "userid": {
                "type": "string
            }
        },
        "required": ["userid"]
    }
}

-> server defines the contract anfd client supplies the arguments

1. initialize  
2. Client sends tools/list (paginated via cursor)
3. Gets back tool definiton  - name, description, input scheme and output schema
4. The client fills these up and calls the server with these filled up fields and fire a call
5. Lastly and most importantly: the server returns the result.content array plus isError
6. In case the server declared the tools.listChanged : true, during the initialization
itcan later push a notifications/ tools/list_changed as well and the client should refetch
the list. 
6. Malformed calls come back error object inside result

-> why is sampling usrful:
1. It lets the server request a LLM Completion from the cliennt's model via sampling

-> How does MCP handle backward compatibility?

1. Capability negotiation ia s progressive feature addition: 
a. Client/server pair only when both sides declares at initialize. 
2. The transport level trnasition is a compatibility dance:
a. Client first POSTs an initializerequest to the server url: if that fails with 4xx, ot falls
back to issuing a GET expecting old-style SSE endpoint event. 
b. Protocol version is carried by MCP-Protocol-Version HTTP Header on every request after
initialzation: so a server can behave differently per negotiated version.

*/


